{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.MahlerJensen","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.MahlerJensen","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Polynomial.MahlerMeasure","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrals.PosLogEqCircleAverage","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Polynomial.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Polynomial.Vieta","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Bounds","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.GroupWithZero.Multiset"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_le_sum_int","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_le_sum_int","kind":"theorem","line":286,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L286-L296","doc":"**The coefficient bound over `ℤ`.**  For a non-zero integer polynomial, the Mahler\nmeasure of its complex image is at most the sum of the absolute values of its coefficients.\n\nThis is the form the Roth's-lemma height bookkeeping consumes: over `ℤ` the `max 1` of\n`mahlerMeasure_le_max_one_sum_norm_coeff` disappears.  ⚠ Still not Landau — see the\nmodule header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.one_le_sum_norm_coeff_map_int","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.one_le_sum_norm_coeff_map_int","kind":"theorem","line":266,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L266-L284","doc":"A non-zero integer polynomial has coefficient sum at least one: its leading coefficient\nis a non-zero integer, hence of absolute value at least one, and every other term of the sum\nis non-negative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_le_sum_norm_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_le_sum_norm_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L260-L264","doc":"When the coefficient sum is itself at least one, the `max` drops away. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_le_max_one_sum_norm_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_le_max_one_sum_norm_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":221,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L221-L258","doc":"**A Landau-*type* bound:** `M(p) ≤ max(1, ‖p‖₁)`.\n\n⚠ Weaker than Landau's inequality — see the module header.  This is the sup bound on the\ncircle, `M(p) = exp ∫ log‖p‖ ≤ sup‖p‖ ≤ ‖p‖₁`, not the Parseval one.\n\n⚠ **The `max 1` is not slack, it is forced.**  The pointwise step is `log ‖p(z)‖ ≤ log S`\nwith `S = ‖p‖₁`, and that **fails** at a zero of `p` on the unit circle when `S < 1`:\nMathlib's `Real.log 0 = 0`, which exceeds `log S < 0`.  Bounding by `max 1 S` makes the\npointwise inequality hold at every point of the circle and so avoids an a.e. argument.\n\nFor a non-zero **integer** polynomial the distinction vanishes, since the leading\ncoefficient alone contributes at least one — that is `mahlerMeasure_le_sum_int`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.norm_eval_le_sum_norm_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.norm_eval_le_sum_norm_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L213-L219","doc":"On the unit circle a polynomial is bounded by the sum of the norms of its\ncoefficients. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.norm_coeff_le_two_pow_mul_mahlerMeasure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.norm_coeff_le_two_pow_mul_mahlerMeasure","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L193-L206","doc":"**The reverse bridge:** `H(p) ≤ 2^{deg p} · M(p)`, one coefficient at a time.\n\nSince `H(p)` is the supremum of `‖coeff k p‖` over `k`, this *is* the height bound; stating it\nper coefficient avoids having to fix a definition of `H` and is the form Gelfond consumes.\n\nNo hypothesis on `k`: above the degree the coefficient is zero and the bound is free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.norm_coeff_le_choose_mul_mahlerMeasure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.norm_coeff_le_choose_mul_mahlerMeasure","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L173-L191","doc":"**The binomial form of the reverse bridge:** `‖coeff k p‖ ≤ C(n, n−k) · M(p)`, `n = deg p`.\n\nVieta writes `coeff k p` as `lead p` times an elementary symmetric function of the roots; the\nJensen form writes `M(p)` as `‖lead p‖` times `∏ max(1,‖root‖)`.  The two share the factor\n`‖lead p‖`, so what is left is exactly `Multiset.norm_esymm_le`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Multiset.norm_esymm_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Multiset.norm_esymm_le","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L128-L171","doc":"The `j`-th elementary symmetric function of a multiset of complex numbers is bounded by\n`C(card, j)` times `∏ max(1, ‖a‖)`.\n\nTwo slacknesses, both deliberate.  A subset product `∏_{a ∈ t} ‖a‖` is bounded by\n`∏_{a ∈ t} max(1,‖a‖)` termwise, and then that is extended from `t` to all of `s` — legal\nprecisely because every extra factor is `≥ 1`, so enlarging the index set only increases the\nproduct.  With every summand bounded by the same `∏_{a ∈ s} max(1,‖a‖)`, the sum over\n`powersetCard j s` costs a factor `card (powersetCard j s) = C(card s, j)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Multiset.norm_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Multiset.norm_prod","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L123-L126","doc":"The norm of a multiset product is the product of the norms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_C_mul_X_sub_C","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_C_mul_X_sub_C","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L105-L116","doc":"**`M(qX − a) = max(‖q‖, ‖a‖)`.**\n\nThis is the shape the `m = 1` case of Roth's lemma measures a rational `a/q` against, and\nthe bean recorded it as absent.  It is the Jensen form at a single root: `qX − a` is\n`C q * (X − C (a/q))`, so its measure is `‖q‖ · max(1, ‖a‖/‖q‖) = max(‖q‖, ‖a‖)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_eq_leadingCoeff_mul_prod_roots","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_eq_leadingCoeff_mul_prod_roots","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L84-L103","doc":"**The Jensen form of the Mahler measure:** `M(P) = ‖lead P‖ · ∏ max(1, ‖root‖)`, the\nproduct running over the roots of `P` with multiplicity.\n\nAssembly rather than analysis, exactly as predicted: `mahlerMeasure_X_sub_C` for a single\nfactor, Mathlib's `prod_mahlerMeasure_eq_mahlerMeasure_prod` for the product, and the\nfactorization `C (lead P) * ∏ (X − aᵢ) = P` which holds over `ℂ` because it is algebraically\nclosed.\n\nNo `P ≠ 0` hypothesis is needed: at `P = 0` the left side is `0` by definition and the right\nside is `‖0‖ * 1 = 0`, since `(0 : ℂ[X]).roots` is the empty multiset. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_X_sub_C","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mahlerMeasure_X_sub_C","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/MahlerJensen.lean#L64-L80","doc":"**The Mahler measure of a linear factor:** `M(X − a) = max(1, ‖a‖)`.\n\nThis is Jensen's formula for a single root.  Mathlib supplies the analytic content\n(`circleAverage_log_norm_sub_const_eq_posLog`) but does not connect it to `mahlerMeasure`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}